            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "Every state requires drivers to carry some level of liability insurance, and the minimums vary widely. You should look up the specific minimum required in your state through your state's Department of Motor Vehicles or Department of Insurance website. However, state minimums are almost universally inadequate for the typical financial losses that result from a serious accident. A hospitalization for a single injured passenger can easily exceed $100,000. A modern vehicle replacement can exceed $40,000. If your liability coverage runs out, the injured party can sue you personally for the remaining balance, putting your wages, savings, and future earnings at risk. Most financial professionals recommend carrying liability limits of at least 100/300/100, regardless of state minimums."
