            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "Term life insurance is exactly what it sounds like: coverage for a fixed period of time, typically 10, 20, or 30 years. You pay a level premium during the term, and if you die during that period, the insurer pays your beneficiaries the policy amount, called the death benefit. If you outlive the term, the policy expires with no payout. Term life is inexpensive because the insurer is only on the hook for the years you are statistically least likely to die. A healthy 30-year-old can typically purchase a 20-year, $500,000 term policy for $20 to $30 per month."
